<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/intermediate/marketing-fundamentals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The previous lesson chose whom to serve; this one runs the machine. Marketing is
    <strong>matching value to the people who want it, profitably</strong> — and profitability
    is the part amateurs forget. The <strong>4Ps</strong> structure the tactical decisions;
    the <strong>funnel</strong> locates where prospective customers leak away; the
    <strong>LTV/CAC ratio</strong> judges whether the whole machine can pay for itself. A
    firm can survive a mediocre product briefly and weak positioning for a while, but it
    cannot survive a marketing system that spends more acquiring customers than those
    customers are worth — which is why the economics come last on this page and first in
    practice.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="toolkit, funnel, economics, and the one asset you own" widget="equationStep" config={mktCfg} />

  <h2>The marketing mix: four decisions that must agree</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>4Ps</strong> are the classic decision grid. <strong>Product</strong> is what
    the customer actually receives, and it has layers: the <em>core</em> benefit (a drill
    is hired for a hole), the <em>actual</em> product (features, design, quality, brand)
    and the <em>augmented</em> product (warranty, service, community) — layers where
    differentiation usually lives once core benefits commoditise. Products also have
    lives: the <strong>product life cycle</strong> (introduction, growth, maturity,
    decline) changes the right strategy at each stage — heavy promotion investment during
    introduction, harvesting efficiency during maturity, managed exit or reinvention at
    decline. <strong>Price</strong> is the only P that earns money rather than spends it,
    and it signals as much as it charges. The two launch strategies sit at opposite ends:
    <em>penetration pricing</em> (enter low, buy share fast, raise later — works when
    scale itself is the advantage) versus <em>price skimming</em> (enter high for the
    eager minority, step down as they're exhausted — the standard play for novel
    electronics, where early adopters pay a premium for time). Underneath both sits the
    deep choice between <em>cost-plus</em> pricing (cost plus margin — simple, ignores the
    customer) and <em>value-based</em> pricing (what the job is worth to the buyer —
    harder, and how nearly all high-margin products are priced). Hermann Simon, the
    dean of pricing practice, put it bluntly: most firms systematically underprice, because
    cost is knowable and value requires research.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Place</strong> is distribution: how the product physically (or digitally)
    reaches the buyer. A <em>direct</em> channel (your own store, your website) keeps
    margin and customer data but limits reach; each added <em>intermediary</em> —
    wholesaler, retailer, marketplace — extends reach and takes a cut (a 40 per cent
    retail margin is normal in consumer goods, which is why "shelf space" is fought over
    and why direct-to-consumer brands boomed once the internet removed the toll booths).
    Channel design also <em>is</em> positioning: luxury brands restrict distribution
    deliberately, because being everywhere is incompatible with being rare.
    <strong>Promotion</strong> is how customers come to know and want it — advertising,
    sales promotion (discounts, bundles), personal selling, direct marketing, public
    relations, sponsorship. The classic organising frame is <strong>AIDA</strong>:
    move the customer through Attention, Interest, Desire, Action — every message should
    know which stage it is addressing. And the <em>push versus pull</em> split decides
    where effort goes: <em>push</em> promotes to the channel (trade discounts, sales
    reps) so retailers stock and recommend you; <em>pull</em> promotes to end customers
    so they arrive asking for you. Strong brands are pull machines — retailers must stock
    them because customers demand them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The grid's real lesson is <strong>consistency</strong>: the four decisions must tell
    the same story. A premium product, sold through discount chains, with downmarket
    promotion and a luxury price is four signals contradicting each other — customers
    resolve contradictions downward. Audi and Rolls-Royce differ not in having "a price"
    but in four coordinated Ps; so do a budget airline and a flag carrier.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the cheapest price wins the market. Price cuts are
    easy for competitors to copy and margin-expensive to sustain; differentiation that
    customers value is neither. Firms compete on price when they have nothing else —
    and price wars in oligopolies (airlines, supermarkets) routinely destroy the profits
    of every participant. Price is a position, not just a number.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked value-pricing calculation shows why value-based thinking pays. A logistics
    company's software saves a mid-size warehouse about 4,000 a month in labour and error
    costs. Cost-plus logic (development cost amortised plus 40 per cent) might suggest
    300 a month — leaving 3,700 of created value with the customer. Value-based logic
    prices at 1,200 — still leaving the customer 2,800 of net benefit, a compelling deal,
    while quadrupling revenue. The negotiation is then about <em>sharing</em> the value,
    not recovering the cost, and the whole evidence apparatus of modern sales (case
    studies, ROI calculators, pilot measurements) exists to prove the 4,000 is real.
    Pricing, done properly, is applied measurement of customer value — and the difference
    between the two prices is why the same company can be poor or rich with identical
    costs.
  </p>

  <h2>The funnel: finding the leak</h2>
  <p>
    The funnel describes the journey from stranger to customer as stages that each lose
    people: awareness → interest → consideration → purchase (and, in modern versions,
    retention and referral). Its power is diagnostic. Work an example: 100,000 people see
    an advert; 10,000 visit the site (10 per cent click-through); 600 add to basket
    (6 per cent); 300 buy (50 per cent checkout completion); 90 are still customers a year
    later. Five numbers, five different diseases. The instinctive fix — "spend more on
    ads" — attacks the <em>first</em> stage and ignores that this funnel loses 97 per cent
    of its visitors before purchase; doubling the checkout completion rate (50 → 65 per
    cent) adds 90 customers at near-zero cost, while doubling ad spend costs a fortune for
    10,000 more visitors of whom 300 buy. <strong>Fix the narrowest stage first</strong> is
    the whole discipline; shouting louder at the top of a leaky funnel is how companies
    spend their way to bankruptcy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Budgets deserve a word, because the funnel rarely gets one. The folk method —
    <em>percentage of last year's sales</em> — quietly inverts cause and effect (marketing
    drives sales, then is budgeted from sales) and cuts spending exactly when sales fall,
    which is when share is cheapest to buy. The professional method is
    <em>objective-and-task</em>: define the target (acquire 5,000 customers at CAC 30),
    cost the tasks that deliver it, budget that. It forces the organisation to price its
    own growth ambitions instead of deriving them from habit.
  </p>

  <h2>CAC and LTV: whether the machine can run</h2>
  <p>
    Two numbers decide whether marketing is an investment or a bonfire.
    <strong>Customer acquisition cost (CAC)</strong> is everything spent to win one
    customer — ad spend, promotions, sales commissions — divided by customers won. If a
    campaign spent 12,000 and won 400 customers, CAC = 30. <strong>Lifetime value
    (LTV)</strong> is the total profit a customer generates: for a subscription, a standard
    approximation is LTV = average revenue per period × gross margin ÷ churn rate. A
    customer paying 20 a month at 70 per cent gross margin with 2.5 per cent monthly churn
    is worth 20 × 0.70 ÷ 0.025 = 560. Against CAC 30, that is a superb machine; against
    CAC 300 with the same LTV, every new customer is a slow loss. Practitioners' rule of
    thumb: <strong>LTV/CAC of 3 or better</strong>, with CAC recovered within about a
    year — lower and growth burns cash, far higher and you are probably underinvesting.
    Two corollaries follow. Retention is the multiplier: cutting churn from 2.5 to 1.9
    per cent lifts that LTV from 560 to about 737 — a retention improvement is worth more
    than most acquisition campaigns, and a retention problem cannot be fixed with more
    acquisition. And measurement is genuinely hard: customers meet brands across many
    touchpoints, so <em>attribution</em> (which ad deserves the credit?) is contested —
    the honest standards are experiments: geographic rollouts, holdout groups that don't
    see the ads, and incrementality tests that measure what marketing <em>changed</em>
    rather than what it touched.
  </p>

  <h2>Channels are rented, brands are owned</h2>
  <p>
    Every marketing channel except one is somebody else's asset. Paid ads stop when the
    spend stops; search rankings move with an algorithm update; a marketplace or social
    platform can change its rules, its reach or its fees overnight (platforms are
    rational to do so once your customers have nowhere else to go). The one asset the
    customer carries in their own head — the stored trust, memory and preference that is
    <strong>brand</strong> — compounds: brand-driven customers convert better, cost less
    to acquire (more direct and word-of-mouth traffic), forgive more mistakes and pay
    more for the same function. This is why content and community, which keep earning
    after publication, are treated as owned assets rather than rent — and why mature
    firms track the <em>share of customers arriving without being paid for</em> as
    seriously as any campaign metric. It is also why the sharpest question to ask of any
    growth story is: of the customers gained last quarter, how many would still have
    arrived if every channel had been switched off? The honest answer, in most businesses,
    is humbling — and the gap between the number and the growth story is exactly what
    brand building is for.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Strategy at the portfolio level gets its own classic tool. The <strong>Boston
    matrix</strong> plots every product a firm owns on market growth (vertical) against
    market share (horizontal), and the four quadrants have stuck for fifty years:
    <em>stars</em> (high share, fast-growing market — invest aggressively), <em>cash
    cows</em> (high share, slow market — milk to fund the rest), <em>question marks</em>
    (low share, fast market — bet or kill, they eat cash either way) and <em>dogs</em>
    (low share, slow market — divest). The matrix's strategic point is that marketing
    budgets are portfolio decisions: cash cows fund question marks, and a firm feeding
    every product equally is hiding its real choices from itself. Its known limits are
    equally instructive — "market share" as a proxy for strength fails in fast-moving
    categories, and the matrix says nothing about synergies between products.
  </p>
  <p>
    Four more extensions. <strong>Services add three Ps</strong> (Booms and Bitner, 1981):
    because services are produced and consumed together and can't be stocked, <em>people</em>
    (whoever delivers the service <em>is</em> the product at that moment — the waiter, the
    nurse, the support engineer), <em>process</em> (queues, booking flows, reliability)
    and <em>physical evidence</em> (the tangible cues — the menu's weight, the clinic's
    cleanliness) carry the differentiation the product itself can't. <strong>Pricing
    psychology</strong> is a measured field: reference prices and anchors (a 90 "was"
    price changes what 60 feels like), decoys (the middle option that makes the target
    option look right), and loss aversion (free trials convert because cancellation feels
    like a loss) — Thaler and Sunstein's nudges, running your pricing page.
    <strong>The privacy reset</strong> is the live structural change: cookie deprecation
    and platform privacy changes have broken the precise tracking that made digital
    advertising scientific, pushing budgets back toward brand, context and first-party
    data — a genuine reversal after two decades. And the <strong>open question</strong>:
    generative AI is collapsing the cost of producing "content" to near zero, which
    floods every channel and makes the scarce assets attention, trust and taste — the
    pieces of marketing that were never arithmetic to begin with.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kotler &amp; Keller, Marketing Management; Hermann Simon, Confessions of the Pricing Man; Booms &amp; Bitner (1981) on services; Thaler &amp; Sunstein, Nudge; CAC/LTV practice literature.</p>
</TopicPage>
